  "factCheck": {
    "status": "verified",
    "by": "HeyMariner daily desk",
    "checkedAt": "2026-11-17",
    "notes": "Rule 10(i): 'A vessel engaged in fishing shall not impede the passage of any vessel following a traffic lane.' Rule 8(f)(i): a vessel required not to impede shall, when required by the circumstances, take early action to allow sufficient sea-room. 8(f)(ii): not relieved of this obligation if approaching so as to involve risk of collision. 8(f)(iii): the vessel the passage of which is not to be impeded remains fully obliged to comply with the Rules of Part B when risk of collision exists. Trawler lights (Rule 26(b)): all-round green over white plus sidelights when making way; red sidelight seen = observer on her port side (nightvessel bearing 300). 'Alter early and boldly, or slow down' reflects Rule 8(a),(b),(e) in general terms; no specific direction is prescribed in the script. Scenario is illustrative."
  },
  "meta": {
    "description": "What COLREG Rule 10(i) really means when a trawler crosses your traffic separation scheme lane at night: the fishing vessel must not impede you and must act early, but under Rule 8(f) you remain fully bound by the steering rules once risk of collision exists. What the OOW does, and why 'must not impede' is not the same as 'must give way'.",
